Z||000000||Cooter||11-07-1999||05:30 PM||||I just installed an Air/Fuel ratio gauge in the Duck-mobile. It swings between rich/lean like I expected it to do. Full throttle acceleration and the gauge goes almost full rich. But, high speed acceleration-above 65 mph and the meter starts out high and slowly drops to the lean side of the gauge. If you stay in the throttle, it drops against the peg on the lean side of the gauge.<P>What could cause this? The fuel pump is new, so is the filter but it seems like low fuel pressure. What do you think?<P>Thanks.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||11-08-1999||12:22 PM||||hook up an ecm scanner and see what the O2 sensor is telling it. I'm not particularly concerned what an aftermarket thing is doing.<P>how did you hook the the new toy up?||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||11-08-1999||02:28 PM||||I used this same gauge on my 'Burb with the Holley Projection. With the Holley system, it displays real-time info from the ecm on a laptop. The gauge was dead on what the Holley ECM was showing on the laptop. So, I fugured that it would be close in the Fiero.<P>The gauge has a filter circuit and it ties into the O2 sensor lead, Ignition and grounds.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-09-1999||01:50 AM||||verify it with an ECM scanner on the fiero. also make sure the thing grounds directly to the block. grounding to the body/frame could throw it way the blazes off. I've seen over 1vdc voltage diferentials between body and block with even very heavy grounds that I knew were good. You're looking at a sensor that has less than a 2 volt total operating range. ||||reg||1
